### What are Spliterator characteristics?

Characteristics are integer bit flags returned by `spliterator.characteristics()`. They describe structural and semantic properties of the data source. The stream pipeline inspects these flags before executing operations to determine whether certain steps can be skipped or optimised.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class CharacteristicsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // ArrayList: ORDERED, SIZED, SUBSIZED
        printCharacteristics("ArrayList", new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001")));

        // HashSet: DISTINCT, SIZED
        printCharacteristics("HashSet", new HashSet<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001")));

        // TreeSet: ORDERED, DISTINCT, SORTED, SIZED
        printCharacteristics("TreeSet", new TreeSet<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001")));

        // LinkedHashSet: ORDERED, DISTINCT, SIZED
        printCharacteristics("LinkedHashSet", new LinkedHashSet<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001")));

        // List.of: ORDERED, SIZED, SUBSIZED, IMMUTABLE
        printCharacteristics("List.of", List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001"));
    }

    static void printCharacteristics(String name, Collection<?> coll) {
        Spliterator<?> sp = coll.spliterator();
        int chars = sp.characteristics();
        List<String> flags = new ArrayList<>();

        if ((chars & Spliterator.ORDERED) != 0) flags.add("ORDERED");
        if ((chars & Spliterator.DISTINCT) != 0) flags.add("DISTINCT");
        if ((chars & Spliterator.SORTED) != 0) flags.add("SORTED");
        if ((chars & Spliterator.SIZED) != 0) flags.add("SIZED");
        if ((chars & Spliterator.SUBSIZED) != 0) flags.add("SUBSIZED");
        if ((chars & Spliterator.NONNULL) != 0) flags.add("NONNULL");
        if ((chars & Spliterator.IMMUTABLE) != 0) flags.add("IMMUTABLE");
        if ((chars & Spliterator.CONCURRENT) != 0) flags.add("CONCURRENT");

        System.out.println(name + ": " + flags);
    }
}
```

```text
ArrayList: [ORDERED, SIZED, SUBSIZED]
HashSet: [DISTINCT, SIZED]
TreeSet: [ORDERED, DISTINCT, SORTED, SIZED]
LinkedHashSet: [ORDERED, DISTINCT, SIZED]
List.of: [ORDERED, SIZED, SUBSIZED, IMMUTABLE]
```

### How do characteristics enable stream optimisations?

The stream pipeline reads characteristics before executing operations. If the source already satisfies a property, the corresponding stream operation becomes a no-op.

| Stream operation | Optimisation | Required characteristic |
|---|---|---|
| sorted() | Skipped (already sorted) | SORTED |
| distinct() | Skipped (already distinct) | DISTINCT |
| toArray() | Pre-allocates exact array | SIZED |
| count() | Returns estimateSize directly | SIZED |
| forEachOrdered() | Must maintain order | ORDERED |
| unordered() | Can drop ordering guarantee | Removes ORDERED |

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.Set;
import java.util.TreeSet;

public class StreamOptimisationDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // TreeSet is SORTED + DISTINCT
        Set<String> accounts = new TreeSet<>(Set.of("FD4001", "SAV2001", "CUR3001"));

        // sorted() is a no-op: TreeSet is already SORTED
        // distinct() is a no-op: TreeSet is already DISTINCT
        long count = accounts.stream()
            .sorted()     // no-op (SORTED)
            .distinct()   // no-op (DISTINCT)
            .count();     // uses SIZED: returns estimateSize directly

        System.out.println("Count: " + count);

        // ArrayList is SIZED: count() is O(1)
        var list = java.util.List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "FD4001");
        long listCount = list.stream().count(); // O(1): returns size directly
        System.out.println("List count: " + listCount);
    }
}
```

```text
Count: 3
List count: 3
```

### How do stream operations change characteristics?

Stream intermediate operations can add or remove characteristics as data flows through the pipeline.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Spliterator;
import java.util.stream.Stream;
import java.util.stream.StreamSupport;

public class CharacteristicsChangeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> accounts = List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "FD4001", "SAV2001");

        // Original: ORDERED, SIZED, SUBSIZED, IMMUTABLE
        System.out.println("Source SIZED: " + accounts.spliterator().hasCharacteristics(Spliterator.SIZED));

        // After filter: SIZED is REMOVED (count unknown after filtering)
        Stream<String> filtered = accounts.stream().filter(a -> a.startsWith("SAV"));
        Spliterator<String> filteredSp = filtered.spliterator();
        System.out.println("After filter SIZED: " + filteredSp.hasCharacteristics(Spliterator.SIZED));

        // After sorted: SORTED is ADDED
        Stream<String> sorted = accounts.stream().sorted();
        Spliterator<String> sortedSp = sorted.spliterator();
        System.out.println("After sorted() SORTED: " + sortedSp.hasCharacteristics(Spliterator.SORTED));

        // After distinct: DISTINCT is ADDED
        Stream<String> distinct = accounts.stream().distinct();
        Spliterator<String> distinctSp = distinct.spliterator();
        System.out.println("After distinct() DISTINCT: " + distinctSp.hasCharacteristics(Spliterator.DISTINCT));
    }
}
```

```text
Source SIZED: true
After filter SIZED: false
After sorted() SORTED: true
After distinct() DISTINCT: true
```

### Which collections have which characteristics?

| Collection | ORDERED | SIZED | SORTED | DISTINCT | SUBSIZED | IMMUTABLE |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| ArrayList | Yes | Yes | No | No | Yes | No |
| LinkedList | Yes | Yes | No | No | No | No |
| HashSet | No | Yes | No | Yes | No | No |
| LinkedHashSet | Yes | Yes | No | Yes | No | No |
| TreeSet | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | No | No |
| List.of() | Yes | Yes | No | No | Yes | Yes |
| ConcurrentHashMap | No | No | No | Yes | No | No |

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake: Expecting parallel forEachOrdered to be fast

```java
// WRONG assumption: forEachOrdered is as fast as forEach in parallel
accounts.parallelStream()
    .forEachOrdered(System.out::println); // must wait for correct order!
// Parallel threads must coordinate to maintain ORDERED sequence.
// This often makes parallel forEachOrdered SLOWER than sequential.
```

```java
// CORRECT: use forEach if order does not matter
accounts.parallelStream()
    .forEach(System.out::println); // unordered, faster
```

#### Mistake: Calling sorted() on an already-sorted TreeSet stream

```java
// INEFFICIENT but not wrong: the stream recognises SORTED and skips the sort
new TreeSet<>(accounts).stream()
    .sorted()   // no-op because source is SORTED
    .toList();
// But it is confusing to readers. Remove the redundant sorted() call.
```

### What about ORDERED and parallel streams?

The ORDERED characteristic affects how parallel streams process and merge results. If a source is ORDERED (like ArrayList), the stream maintains encounter order even in parallel. This requires coordination between threads, which can reduce parallelism benefits.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.HashSet;

public class OrderedParallelDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> orderedSource = List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "FD4001");
        Set<String> unorderedSource = new HashSet<>(orderedSource);

        // ORDERED source: limit() must return first N in encounter order
        System.out.println("Ordered limit(2): " +
            orderedSource.parallelStream().limit(2).toList());

        // Unordered source: limit() can return any N elements
        System.out.println("Unordered limit(2): " +
            unorderedSource.parallelStream().limit(2).toList());
    }
}
```

```text
Ordered limit(2): [SAV2001, CUR3001]
Unordered limit(2): [CUR3001, FD4001]
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **SUBSIZED for balanced splitting.** SUBSIZED means that splits produce Spliterators with known sizes. ArrayList reports SUBSIZED because splitting by index range always produces known-size halves. This enables the ForkJoinPool to create balanced work distribution.
- **Custom Spliterator characteristics.** When implementing a custom Spliterator, report accurate characteristics. Over-reporting (claiming SORTED when not sorted) causes bugs. Under-reporting (not claiming SIZED when size is known) prevents optimisations.
- **Stream.unordered().** Calling `stream.unordered()` removes the ORDERED characteristic, allowing the pipeline to use order-independent optimisations like faster distinct() with ConcurrentHashMap.
- **Characteristics propagation.** The stream pipeline tracks characteristics through each intermediate operation. Understanding this explains why count() is O(1) on a sized stream but O(n) after a filter().

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What does SIZED enable?" : Pre-allocation of result arrays in toArray(), O(1) count(), and balanced parallel splitting.
- "What does SORTED enable?" : The stream skips the sorted() operation because the source is already in order.
- "Why does filter() remove SIZED?" : Because the number of elements passing the filter is unknown at pipeline construction time.
- "What is the difference between ORDERED and SORTED?" : ORDERED means elements have a defined encounter order (like insertion order). SORTED means elements are in a comparator-defined order. A LinkedHashSet is ORDERED but not SORTED. A TreeSet is both.
